<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George Westinghouse</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1846">1846</a>–<a href="/years/1914">1914</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as George Westinghouse Jr.</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">George Westinghouse was an American inventor, engineer and industrialist based in Pittsburgh, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, best known for the railway air brake and as a pioneer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alternating-current">alternating current</a> (AC) electrical power distribution. He founded the Westinghouse Air Brake Company in 1869 and Union Switch &amp; Signal in 1881, and developed means for the safe production and transmission of natural gas. His Westinghouse Electric Corporation, founded in 1886, competed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-edison">Thomas Edison</a>'s direct current, lit the White City at the 1893 Columbian Exposition in Chicago and opened the first large-scale AC generating plant at Niagara Falls in 1895. He received 360 patents and established 61 companies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-westinghouse.jpg" alt="George Westinghouse" width="330" height="433" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown (Life time: subject died before 1914) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:George_Westinghouse_1884.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>George Westinghouse was born on 6 October 1846 in Central Bridge, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, the son of Emeline Vedder and George Westinghouse Sr., a farmer and machine shop owner. His family came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>, and the name was anglicized from Wistinghausen. He showed a talent for machinery and business from his youth and worked in his father's workshop. In June 1863 his parents allowed him to enlist, and he later served in the Union Navy as an engineer on the gunboats USS Muscoota and USS Stars and Stripes. After his discharge in August 1865 he enrolled at Union College but dropped out in his first term.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Brakemen of the time ran along the tops of the cars applying brakes by hand, which limited trains to ten cars and killed or maimed thousands a year. In 1869, aged 23, Westinghouse demonstrated in Pittsburgh a braking system using compressed air with a single pipe along the train. A rupture left the train without brakes, so his engineers inverted the process over the next two years, using valves so that constant pressure kept the brakes off and an air reservoir on each car applied them if the line broke. In May 1884 he capped a gas blowout on his own estate, and by 1886 his Philadelphia Company owned 58 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> and 184 miles of piping.</p><p>In 1885 he learned of the Gaulard-Gibbs transformer, which let <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alternating-current">alternating current</a> be stepped up for long-distance transmission, and saw AC as the basis of a competitive system against Edison's low-voltage direct current. In the &quot;war of currents&quot; Edison promoted the perception that high-voltage AC was deadly, and in 1890 the first electric chair was powered with a Westinghouse generator. The war ended in 1892 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-p-morgan">J. P. Morgan</a> forced Edison General Electric to adopt AC. Westinghouse also acquired the rights to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikola-tesla">Nikola Tesla</a>'s brushless induction motor and polyphase patents.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Westinghouse was the first industrial employer in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to give workers a five-and-a-half day week, from June 1881. At Wilmerding, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, workers' houses had running water, electricity and gas, and there were pension and insurance systems. His engineers kept their names on patents. He lost control of his companies in 1907 and severed all ties in 1911, the year he received the AIEE Edison Medal. He died on 12 March 1914. A memorial funded by 50,000 of his employees was placed in Schenley Park in 1930.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “George Westinghouse” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
